    "> in freecad, when you discretize a wire, does it divide it into that many subsegments of equal length?",
    "No, `discretize()` in FreeCAD does **not** create equal-length segments. It creates points at **equal parameter intervals** along the curve.\n\n## How FreeCAD Discretize Works:\n\n```python\nwire.discretize(Number=200)\n```\n\nThis:\n✅ **Divides parameter space** into 200 equal intervals (0 to 1)\n✅ **Evaluates curve** at each parameter value\n❌ **Does NOT ensure equal arc lengths** between points\n\n## Parameter vs Arc Length:\n\n- **Parameter spacing**: Equal steps in mathematical parameter (0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.015...)\n- **Arc length spacing**: Equal physical distances along the curve\n\n## Why This Matters:\n\nIn areas of **high curvature** (like leading edge), points will be **closer together** in physical space even though they're equally spaced in parameter space.\n\nIn areas of **low curvature** (like flat sections), points will be **farther apart** physically.\n\n## For Equal Arc Length:\n\nYou'd need a different approach:\n```python\n# Hypothetical equal arc length discretization\ntotal_length = wire.Length\nsegment_length = total_length / 200\npoints = []\nfor i in range(200):\n    distance = i * segment_length\n    point = wire.valueAt(wire.getParameterByLength(distance))\n    points.append(point)\n```\n\nBut FreeCAD's standard `discretize()` uses **equal parameter intervals**, not equal arc lengths.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> for a straight line, they're equal spacing, but for curves, tighter together",
    "Exactly! You've got it:\n\n## Parameter vs Physical Spacing:\n\n**Straight line**: Parameter spacing = Physical spacing\n- Equal parameter steps → Equal distance steps\n- Points evenly distributed along the line\n\n**Curved sections**: Parameter spacing ≠ Physical spacing  \n- Equal parameter steps → **Unequal distance steps**\n- Points **bunch up** in high-curvature areas (leading edge)\n- Points **spread out** in low-curvature areas (flat sections)\n\n## For Your Airfoil:\n\n- **Leading edge radius**: Points will be **tighter together** (high curvature)\n- **Flat top section**: Points will be **farther apart** (low curvature)  \n- **Trailing edge**: Points **tighter together** (sharp transition)\n- **Bottom surface**: **Variable spacing** based on curvature\n\n## Impact on Control Point Clustering:\n\nThis means your control point **indices** don't correspond to **equal arc lengths** along the airfoil perimeter. The clustering by index ranges (0-39, 40, 41-80) gives you **parameter-based segments**, not **equal-length segments**.\n\nFor morphing, this is actually **good** because:\n- **More control** where the curve changes rapidly (leading edge)\n- **Less control** where the curve is simple (flat sections)\n- **Natural distribution** matches the geometric complexity\n[Tool uses: none]",
